{{also|bonâ fide}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[bonâ fide]]

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˌbəʊ.nəˈfaɪ.di/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˌboʊnəˈfaɪdi/|/ˈboʊnə.faɪd/|/ˈboʊnəˌfiːdeɪ/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-bona-fide 2.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}, {{audio|en-us-bona-fide.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-us-bona fide.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

====Usage notes====
The pronunciation {{IPA|/ˈboʊnə.faɪd/|lang=en}}, is the most common one in the USA and therefore listed first in American dictionaries, incl. American Heritage, Merriam-Webster, and the American version of Collins.<ref>Webster's Third New International Dictionary, Unabridged</ref>

===Etymology===
From the {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|bonā}} {{m|la|fidē}} (“in good faith”) which is the [[ablative case]] of {{m|la|bona}} {{m|la|fidēs}} (“good faith”).

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# In [[good]] [[faith]].
#: ''Although he failed, the prime minister made a '''bona fide''' attempt to repair the nation's damaged economy.''
# [[genuine|Genuine]]; not [[counterfeit]].
#: ''This is a '''bona fide''' Roman coin.''
#* '''2000''', ''[http://www.imdb.com/title/tt0190590/quotes O Brother Where Art Thou?]'' (movie):
#*: Ulysses Everett McGill: I am the only daddy you got! I’m the damn pater familias!
#*: Wharvey Gal: But you ain’t '''bona fide'''!

====Usage notes====
Sometimes misspelled as *''bonafied,'' by incorrectly analyzing as the past tense of assumed *''bonafy''.<ref>[http://www.wsu.edu/~brians/errors/bonafied.html Bonafied / Bona Fide], Paul Brians</ref>

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|done in good faith}} [[sincere]]
* {{sense|genuine}} [[authentic]], [[genuine]]

====Antonyms====
* {{sense|done in bad faith}} [[mala fide]]

